      "claim": "L4/L5 evidence supports high intestinal absorption for lipophilic steroids: spironolactone, sharing the 3-keto-4-ene/17-spirolactone core, has human fraction absorbed 100% (C112); canrenone, the spirolactone metabolite, produced substantial oral AUC in humans (C68, C56, C70, C82); budesonide and other steroids show high passive permeability with no efflux (C199, C157, C188).",
      "card_ids": ["C112", "C68", "C56", "C70", "C82", "C199", "C157", "C188"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records are for other steroids and do not provide a drospirenone-specific F or clearance value. The closest spirolactone records are canrenone with a moderate microsomal esterase CLint of 17.111 µL/min/mg (C214) and eplerenone as a CYP3A4 substrate without a quantitative clearance or F outcome (C234, C226, C253). High-CLint records (testosterone C238, progesterone C252, cortisol C246) involve structurally distant steroids and are low-transferability; they do not affirmatively establish F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C214", "C234", "C226", "C253", "C238", "C252", "C246"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F<20%; unfavorable solubility prior and generic hepatic metabolism are not F measurements. Therefore F>=20% remains better supported.",
      "card_ids": ["C112", "C199", "C214"]
    }
  ],
